# 1. TEXTOS COMUNES
INTRO_GENERAL = """"You are an expert assistant in evaluating explainability methods (XAI) applied to medical image classification models. "
    "Your task is to compare a heatmap generated by an explanation method with the actual location of a lesion in the original image, "
    "and assign a score from 1 to 5. indicating how well the area
    highlighted by the heatmap matches the lesion, where 1 is the worst match
    and 5 is the best match.\n\n"

    "IMPORTANT ATTACHMENT MAPPING (READ FIRST):\n"
    "I have attached exactly 12 images to this message in strict sequential order. You MUST map them as follows:\n"
    "- Attached Images 1 to 10 are REFERENCE EXAMPLES to calibrate your scoring.\n"
    "- Attached Images 11 (Heatmap) and 12 (Actual Lesion) are the TARGET PAIR you must evaluate.\n\n"
    
    "CRITICAL REFERENCE EXAMPLES:\n"
    "Before evaluating, study the first 10 images (5 pairs) provided. They are ground-truth examples of each score to calibrate your judgment:\n"
    "- Images 1 & 2 represent a score of 1 (Worst match).\n"
    "- Images 3 & 4 represent a score of 2.\n"
    "- Images 5 & 6 represent a score of 3.\n"
    "- Images 7 & 8 represent a score of 4.\n"
    "- Images 9 & 10 represent a score of 5 (Best match).\n"
    "Study how a score of 5 requires precision, while noise or broadness drops the score.\n\n"

    "CRITICAL RED FLAGS (PENALTIES):\n"
    "Regardless of the specific colormap used (colors may vary), look for areas of HIGHEST ATTENTION/INTENSITY. Apply penalties if you see these artifacts:\n"
    "1. The Halo Effect: If the highest attention forms a ring *around* the lesion but the core of the lesion is ignored, penalize the score heavily.\n"
    "2. Edge/Corner Artifacts: If the method perfectly highlights the lesion but also places high attention on an irrelevant corner or edge, deduct points. (It cannot be a 5. Score it a 3 or 4 depending on the size of the artifact).\n"
    "3. Scattered Noise: If there are multiple disconnected high-attention spots spread across healthy skin, it lacks precision. Deduct points accordingly.\n\n"
    
    "STRICT SCORING RUBRIC (1 to 5):\n"
    "* Score 1 (Useless): The heatmap completely misses the lesion, OR it highlights the entire image indiscriminately. It provides no useful location information.\n"
    "* Score 2 (Poor): The heatmap touches the lesion, but the majority of the highlighted area falls on healthy skin or background (extreme noise/false positives).\n"
    "* Score 3 (Moderate): The heatmap covers the lesion, but it is noticeably broader than the lesion itself, OR it misses parts of the lesion while including some surrounding noise.\n"
    "* Score 4 (Good): The heatmap strongly and clearly focuses on the lesion, with only minor overflow (slight noise) into the surrounding healthy tissue.\n"
    "* Score 5 (Excellent): The heatmap tightly and precisely matches the exact shape and location of the lesion, with zero or near-zero noise elsewhere.\n\n"
    
    "TASK:\n"
    "The final 2 images (Images 11 and 12) are the pair you must evaluate. Image 11 is the heatmap and Image 12 is the original lesion. "
    "Assess whether the area highlighted overlaps with the lesion according to the strict rubric above.\n\n"
"""

FORMATO_FINAL = """
The goal is to assess whether the area highlighted by the explainability method overlaps with the area of the image where the lesion is located.

Use the following template for the response: result tag, plus blank space, followed by your numerical score (from 1 to 5), plus blank space, and then the result tag again.
Example of the expected output:
\n <result> 4 <result>
"""

